/* =========================================================
   APOSTLES: did they all understand God as revealed in Jesus?
   ========================================================= */

RG.apostlesOverview = [
  "Did all the apostles understand God as he was revealed in Jesus? The honest answer from the New Testament itself is: not all at once. The Gospels show the Twelve misunderstanding Jesus again and again, arguing about who is greatest, wanting fire on enemies and running away at his arrest. Jesus himself said, “I have yet many things to tell you, but you can’t bear them now. However when he, the Spirit of truth, has come, he will guide you into all truth” (John 16:12-13). Understanding was meant to grow.",
  "The book of Acts shows that growth happening. After Pentecost, Peter preaches boldly, yet it takes a vision repeated three times before he will enter a Gentile home and say, “Truly I perceive that God doesn’t show favoritism” (Acts 10:34). The Jerusalem council in Acts 15 is a whole church learning, through debate, testimony and Scripture, that Gentiles do not need to keep the Law of Moses to belong. Even after that, Peter drew back from eating with Gentiles in Antioch (Gal 2:11-14), and the Jerusalem church remained “zealous for the law” (Acts 21:20).",
  "Paul describes his own understanding as a revelation: “I didn’t receive it from man, nor was I taught it, but it came to me through revelation of Jesus Christ” (Gal 1:11-12). God was pleased “to reveal his Son in me” (Gal 1:16). Yet Paul also admits he sees “in a mirror, dimly” (1 Cor 13:12) and says, “Not that I have already obtained” (Phil 3:12). The apostles were real people, walking forward in light that kept growing.",
  "The New Testament also holds different emphases together. Paul stresses grace and faith apart from works of the Law (Rom 3:28). James insists that faith without works is dead (Jas 2:17, 24). John speaks of love and abiding: “God is love” (1 John 4:8). Martin Luther famously struggled with James, calling it “an epistle of straw” in his 1522 preface by comparison with other books. Most Christian traditions read these voices as complementary, describing the same living faith from different angles.",
  "The relational-gospel reading, found in writers like Brad Jersak, Greg Boyd and Brian Zahnd, suggests that the apostles sometimes still carried old-covenant reflexes: harsh language about opponents, the pull of purity rules, or images of divine wrath drawn from earlier Scripture. On this view, Jesus is the measure even of the apostles, and their writings are a faithful but human witness that is itself growing toward the full light of Christ. This reading tries to take seriously how the New Testament shows its own leaders learning and being corrected.",
  "The traditional reading, held by most Catholic, Orthodox, Reformed and evangelical churches, is that while the apostles grew personally and made mistakes in their conduct (like Peter at Antioch), their inspired writings are fully authoritative and harmonious, because the Spirit guided them into all truth as Jesus promised. On this view, apparent tensions are resolved by careful reading, not by ranking texts. Readers can hold both convictions in conversation: the apostles grew, and the Spirit was at work in that growth to give the church a trustworthy witness to Christ."
];

